  "post": {
    "caption": "Queen Victoria's coronation ring was made too small. On June 28, 1838, the royal goldsmiths Rundell, Bridge and Rundell had sized it for her little finger, and the Archbishop of Canterbury forced it onto her ring finger anyway. Her journal says he put it on the wrong finger, and afterward she soaked her hand in iced water to get it off. The ring is a sapphire with rubies set in a cross and a border of diamonds, copied from the 1831 coronation ring of William the Fourth on a smaller scale. King George the Fifth placed it with the Crown Jewels in March 1919.",
